<section class="section" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
<p class="eyebrow">Five perspectives, one team</p><h2 id="team-title">The team</h2>
<p class="intro">We’re M.Sc. Computer Science students at the Agentic Software Engineering, Paderborn University lab. What connects us is curiosity about the systems behind software—and the people who depend on it. We bring different interests, from distributed infrastructure to human-computer interaction, to the same conversation.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Mohammad.png}}" alt="Mohammad"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Full-stack development, gaming</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for ripping through boilerplate and syntax lookups, but it frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without genuinely understanding system architecture or context. Right now, it works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough code review.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">The core job will shift from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Autonomous agents will handle boilerplate, while engineers focus on verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p><div class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Nithya"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI assistants are productivity multipliers for well-understood domains and boilerplate, but break down in complex legacy enterprise codebases. They can be confidently wrong about synchronization primitives and concurrency, so a fast copilot still needs senior oversight.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineering will center on systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus rather than mundane feature programming. Junior roles will evolve into orchestration positions where knowing failure modes and domain invariants matters more than low-level library APIs.</p><div class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Rafee"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">For a fresh developer, AI is an unbelievable interactive tutor that demystifies obscure compiler errors in seconds. But relying on it too early is a trap: understanding the output without understanding the mechanics creates an illusion of competence.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Natural language and intent-driven wireframes will compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will focus on user empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state rather than CSS layout fighting.</p><div class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Raghvendra"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">Current LLMs are incredible for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL or boilerplate endpoints. However, they lack deep situational context and often pull in deprecated methods without warning. Their true utility is reducing initial friction, not making core architectural choices.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Developers will converse with multi-agent pipelines that autonomously build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting business constraints.</p><div class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Sayemah"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping into engineering, making the barrier to entry lower for novel ideas. Software is also human communication and interface empathy. Current models produce functional code, but it often lacks aesthetic intention and elegance.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with high-level design systems and ethical standards, freeing creative energy for user delight and seamless interactions.</p><div class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</div></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="section" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title"><p class="eyebrow">Our shared point of view</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2><div class="future" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
<p class="lede">We see AI changing the mechanics of making software—not removing the need for engineering judgment. As agents take on boilerplate and routine implementation, human work becomes even more important: choosing the right problem, checking the result, and making systems work for people.</p>
<div class="principles"><article class="principle"><b>01 / DEFINE</b><h3>Make intent precise</h3><p>Clear requirements, edge cases, and domain constraints will guide capable coding tools.</p></article><article class="principle"><b>02 / VERIFY</b><h3>Earn trust with evidence</h3><p>Testing, formal methods, code review, and failure analysis remain essential.</p></article><article class="principle"><b>03 / CARE</b><h3>Keep people in the system</h3><p>Accessibility, privacy, thoughtful interfaces, and human oversight should shape what we build.</p></article></div>
<h3 class="forecast-title">Five lenses on what comes next</h3><div class="forecasts">
<article class="forecast"><h4>Mohammad · Architecture and verification</h4><p>Agents may handle boilerplate, shifting engineers toward requirements, architecture, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Nithya · Integration and invariants</h4><p>Systems integration, formal verification, distributed consensus, and failure modes become central concerns.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Rafee · Interfaces from intent</h4><p>Intent-driven wireframes may produce accessible interfaces, giving engineers more time for empathy and performant client state.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Raghvendra · Multi-agent systems</h4><p>Agents may build and benchmark services while engineers define objectives, protect privacy, and reconcile constraints.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Sayemah · Human-centered design</h4><p>Design systems and ethical standards can help engineers steer agents toward multidisciplinary, delightful experiences.</p></article>
</div></div></section>
</main>
<footer><div class="wrap"><p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · Context Engine</p></div></footer>
