<p class="copy">We’re a team of computer scientists exploring how to build software that works well, makes sense, and keeps people in the picture.</p>
<span class="pill">Agentic Software Engineering · Paderborn University</span>
</section>
<section class="section" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
<p class="eyebrow">Five perspectives, one team</p><h2 id="team-title">The team</h2>
<p class="intro">We’re M.Sc. Computer Science students at the Agentic Software Engineering, Paderborn University lab. What connects us is curiosity about the systems behind software—and the people who depend on it. We bring different interests, from distributed infrastructure to human-computer interaction, to the same conversation.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">M</div><div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Full-stack development, gaming</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for ripping through boilerplate and syntax lookups, but it frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up the typing part of programming without genuinely understanding system architecture or context. Right now, it works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough code review.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">The core job will shift almost entirely from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Writing raw boilerplate by hand will largely disappear, replaced by autonomous agents managed by engineers acting as system architects. True engineering value will center on verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build rather than how to type it out.</p>
<div class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</div>
</div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">N</div><div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI assistants are fantastic productivity multipliers for well-understood domains and boilerplate generation, but break down when faced with complex legacy enterprise codebases. They often exhibit blind confidence on subtle synchronization primitives and concurrency issues. Right now, AI acts as a fast copilot, but you cannot let it navigate without senior oversight.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineering will center heavily on systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus rather than mundane feature programming. Junior roles will evolve into system orchestration positions where knowing failure modes and domain invariants will matter far more than knowing low-level library APIs.</p>
<div class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</div>
</div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">From a fresh developer perspective, AI is an unbelievable interactive tutor that demystifies obscure compiler errors in seconds. But relying on it too early is a trap: it creates an illusion of competence where you understand the output without understanding the underlying mechanics.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Natural language and intent-driven wireframes will directly compile into accessible, responsive user interfaces. The frontend engineer's job will focus almost entirely on user empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state rather than CSS layout fighting.</p>
<div class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</div>
</div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">Current LLMs are incredible for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL or boilerplate endpoints. However, they lack deep situational context and often pull in deprecated methods without warning. Their true utility is reducing initial friction, not making core architectural choices.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Developers will primarily converse with multi-agent pipelines that autonomously build, test, and benchmark microservices against synthetic workloads. Humans will intervene primarily to define objective functions, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting business constraints.</p>
<div class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</div>
</div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">S</div><div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping into engineering, making the barrier to entry much lower for novel ideas. However, software is as much about human communication and interface empathy as it is about syntax. Current models produce functional code, but it often lacks aesthetic intention and elegance.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become much more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents using high-level design systems and ethical standards, treating code generation as a utility while spending creative energy on user delight and seamless interactions.</p>
<div class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</div>
</div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="section" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title">
<p class="eyebrow">Our shared point of view</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
<div class="future" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
<p class="lede">We see AI changing the mechanics of making software—not removing the need for engineering judgment. As agents take on more boilerplate and routine implementation, the human work becomes even more important: choosing the right problem, checking the result, and making systems work for people.</p>
<div class="principles">
<article class="principle"><b>01 / DEFINE</b><h3>Make intent precise</h3><p>Clear requirements, edge cases, and domain constraints will guide increasingly capable coding tools.</p></article>
<article class="principle"><b>02 / VERIFY</b><h3>Earn trust with evidence</h3><p>Testing, formal methods, code review, and failure analysis remain essential when generated code can look convincing.</p></article>
<article class="principle"><b>03 / CARE</b><h3>Keep people in the system</h3><p>Accessibility, privacy, thoughtful interfaces, and human oversight should shape how we build—not just what we build.</p></article>
</div>
<h3 class="forecast-title">Five lenses on what comes next</h3>
<div class="forecasts">
<article class="forecast"><h4>Mohammad · Architecture and verification</h4><p>Autonomous agents may handle boilerplate, shifting engineers toward requirements, architectural constraints, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Nithya · Integration and invariants</h4><p>Systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus will make orchestration and failure modes central engineering concerns.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Rafee · Interfaces from intent</h4><p>Intent-driven wireframes may produce accessible interfaces, giving engineers more time for empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client state.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Raghvendra · Multi-agent systems</h4><p>Agents may build and benchmark microservices, while engineers define objectives, protect privacy, and reconcile business constraints.</p></article>
<article class="forecast"><h4>Sayemah · Human-centered design</h4><p>Engineers will steer agents with design systems and ethical standards, creating room for multidisciplinary thinking and delightful experiences.</p></article>
</div></div></section>
</main>
