            <p class="interests"><strong>Into:</strong> Full-stack development and gaming</p>
            <h4>A little about me</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p>
            <h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but can hallucinate subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without truly understanding architecture or context; right now, it’s an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough review.</p>
            <h4>Where we’re headed</h4><p class="detail">Engineering will shift from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Autonomous agents may handle boilerplate, while engineers focus on verification, fault tolerance, and deciding what to build.</p>
            <div class="hot-take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Nithya">
          <div class="member-body">
            <div class="member-topline"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div>
            <p class="interests"><strong>Into:</strong> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, and board games</p>
            <h4>A little about me</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p>
            <h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI assistants are productivity multipliers for well-understood domains and boilerplate, but struggle with complex legacy code. They can be confidently wrong about synchronization and concurrency, so a fast copilot still needs senior oversight.</p>
            <h4>Where we’re headed</h4><p class="detail">Systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus will matter more than routine feature programming. Junior roles may evolve toward system orchestration, where understanding failure modes and domain invariants is essential.</p>
            <div class="hot-take">“AI autocomplete hasn’t eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Rafee">
          <div class="member-body">
            <div class="member-topline"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div>
            <p class="interests"><strong>Into:</strong> Frontend UI/UX, React and Next.js, table tennis, and photography</p>
            <h4>A little about me</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p>
            <h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">For a fresh developer, AI is an unbelievable interactive tutor that demystifies obscure compiler errors in seconds. But relying on it too early can create an illusion of competence: understanding the output isn’t the same as understanding the mechanics.</p>
            <h4>Where we’re headed</h4><p class="detail">Natural-language intent and wireframes could compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will put more energy into user empathy, accessibility, and performant client-side state.</p>
            <div class="hot-take">“If you can’t debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Raghvendra">
          <div class="member-body">
            <div class="member-topline"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div>
            <p class="interests"><strong>Into:</strong> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, and chess</p>
            <h4>A little about me</h4><p class="detail">I will spend four hours automating a manual five-minute task, and call it an investment.</p>
            <h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">Current LLMs are incredible for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL or boilerplate endpoints. But they lack deep situational context and may use deprecated methods without warning. Their value is reducing initial friction, not making core architectural choices.</p>
            <h4>Where we’re headed</h4><p class="detail">Multi-agent pipelines may build, test, and benchmark microservices against synthetic workloads. Engineers will still need to define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting business constraints.</p>
            <div class="hot-take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Sayemah">
          <div class="member-body">
            <div class="member-topline"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div>
            <p class="interests"><strong>Into:</strong> Calligraphy, cooking, traveling, and human-computer interaction</p>
            <h4>A little about me</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p>
            <h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping to engineering, lowering the barrier to trying novel ideas. But software is also human communication and interface empathy: functional code can still lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p>
            <h4>Where we’re headed</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with high-level design systems and ethical standards, leaving more creative energy for user delight and seamless interactions.</p>
            <div class="hot-take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</div>
