<header><div class="wrap head"><div><a class="brand" href="#top">Context Engine</a><p class="tagline">Thoughtful engineering, human by design.</p></div><div class="actions"><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#team">The team</a><a href="#future-of-se">The future</a></nav><button id="theme-toggle" aria-label="Switch to night mode" aria-pressed="false" type="button">☾</button></div></div></header>
<main class="wrap" id="top">
<section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Agentic Software Engineering · Paderborn University</p><h1>Good engineering starts with good questions.</h1><p class="muted">We’re a team of computer scientists exploring how to build software that works well, makes sense, and keeps people in the picture.</p></section>
<section id="team"><p class="eyebrow">Five perspectives, one team</p><h2>The team</h2><p class="intro">We’re M.Sc. Computer Science students at the Agentic Software Engineering, Paderborn University lab. Curiosity about systems and the people who depend on them connects us across interests ranging from distributed infrastructure to human-computer interaction.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">M</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Full-stack development, gaming</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p><h4>AI today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but hallucinates subtle logical flaws and lacks architectural context. It works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough review.</p><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineers will specify requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints while autonomous agents handle boilerplate. Verification, fault tolerance, and deciding what to build will be central.</p><p class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">N</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p><h4>AI today</h4><p class="detail">AI helps with well-understood domains and boilerplate but struggles with complex legacy systems. It can be confidently wrong about synchronization and concurrency, so senior oversight matters.</p><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus will matter more than routine feature programming. Engineers will need to know failure modes and domain invariants.</p><p class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p><h4>AI today</h4><p class="detail">AI is an interactive tutor that can demystify compiler errors, but relying on it too early creates an illusion of competence without understanding the underlying mechanics.</p><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Intent-driven wireframes may compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will focus on empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state.</p><p class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p><h4>AI today</h4><p class="detail">LLMs are useful for rapid prototyping and SQL, but lack situational context and may use deprecated methods. Their value is reducing friction, not making architectural choices.</p><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Multi-agent pipelines may build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting constraints.</p><p class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">S</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p><h4>AI today</h4><p class="detail">AI enables creativity and swift prototyping, but software also requires human communication and interface empathy. Functional code can lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with design systems and ethical standards, making room for user delight.</p><p class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</p></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="section" id="future-of-se"><p class="eyebrow">Our shared view</p><h2>The Future of Software Engineering</h2><div class="future"><p class="muted">AI will change how software is made, not remove the need for judgment. As agents take on routine implementation, engineers will define problems, verify results, and ensure systems serve people.</p><div class="future-grid"><article><b>Define intent</b><p>Requirements, edge cases, and domain constraints guide capable tools.</p></article><article><b>Verify with evidence</b><p>Testing, formal methods, code review, and failure analysis remain essential.</p></article><article><b>Keep people central</b><p>Accessibility, privacy, ethical standards, and thoughtful interfaces shape good software.</p></article><article><b>Build resilient systems</b><p>Integration, distributed systems, orchestration, and fault tolerance will matter.</p></article></div></div></section>
</main>
<footer><div class="wrap"><p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · Context Engine</p></div></footer>
