<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">M</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Full-stack development, gaming</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without understanding architecture or context, so every commit needs thorough review.</p><h4>In 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineers will specify requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints while autonomous agents handle boilerplate. Verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build will define engineering value.</p><p class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">N</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI multiplies productivity for well-understood domains and boilerplate, but can fail in complex legacy systems and be confidently wrong about synchronization or concurrency. Human oversight remains essential.</p><h4>In 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus will matter more than routine feature programming. Engineers will need to understand failure modes and domain invariants.</p><p class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is an interactive tutor that can demystify compiler errors, but relying on it too early risks understanding output without understanding the mechanics.</p><h4>In 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Intent-driven wireframes may compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will focus on user empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state.</p><p class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">LLMs are useful for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL, but lack situational context and may use deprecated methods. They reduce initial friction, not replace architectural choices.</p><h4>In 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Multi-agent pipelines may build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting constraints.</p><p class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="portrait" aria-hidden="true">S</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping, but software also needs human communication and interface empathy. Functional code can lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p><h4>In 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with design systems and ethical standards, leaving more room for user delight.</p><p class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</p></div></article>
</div></section>
