<main class="wrap" id="top">
<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
<p class="eyebrow">Agentic Software Engineering · Paderborn University</p>
<h1 id="hero-title">Good engineering starts with good questions.</h1>
<p class="hero-copy">We’re a team of computer scientists exploring how to build software that works well, makes sense, and keeps people in the picture.</p>
<span class="pill">Five perspectives, one team</span>
</section>

<section class="section" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
<p class="eyebrow">Meet the people behind the ideas</p>
<h2 id="team-title">The team</h2>
<p class="intro">We’re M.Sc. Computer Science students at the Agentic Software Engineering, Paderborn University lab. Curiosity about software systems and the people who depend on them connects us across interests from distributed infrastructure to human-computer interaction.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card">
<img class="portrait" src="{{image:Mohammad.png}}" alt="Mohammad">
<div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div>
<p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Full-stack development, gaming</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without understanding system architecture or context. Right now, it works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough code review.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">The core job will shift from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Autonomous agents may handle boilerplate, while engineers focus on verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p>
<p class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</p>
</div></article>

<article class="card">
<img class="portrait" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Nithya">
<div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div>
<p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI assistants are productivity multipliers for well-understood domains and boilerplate, but struggle with complex legacy enterprise codebases. They can be confidently wrong about synchronization and concurrency, so a fast copilot still needs senior oversight.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineering will center on systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus rather than routine feature programming. Engineers will need to know failure modes and domain invariants.</p>
<p class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</p>
</div></article>

<article class="card">
<img class="portrait" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Rafee">
<div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div>
<p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is an unbelievable interactive tutor that demystifies obscure compiler errors in seconds. Relying on it too early can create an illusion of competence: understanding output is not the same as understanding mechanics.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Natural language and intent-driven wireframes may compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will focus on empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state.</p>
<p class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</p>
</div></article>

<article class="card">
<img class="portrait" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Raghvendra">
<div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div>
<p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">Current LLMs are incredible for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL or boilerplate endpoints. They lack deep situational context and can pull in deprecated methods without warning. Their true utility is reducing initial friction, not making core architectural choices.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Developers may converse with multi-agent pipelines that autonomously build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objective functions, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting business constraints.</p>
<p class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</p>
</div></article>

<article class="card">
<img class="portrait" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Sayemah">
<div class="content">
<div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div>
<p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p>
<h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p>
<h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping into engineering, making the barrier to entry lower for novel ideas. Software is also human communication and interface empathy. Current models produce functional code, but it can lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p>
<h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with high-level design systems and ethical standards, leaving creative energy for user delight and seamless interactions.</p>
<p class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</p>
</div></article>
