<header><div class="wrap head"><div><a class="brand" href="#top">Context Engine</a><p class="tagline">Thoughtful engineering, human by design.</p></div><div class="actions"><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#team">The team</a><a href="#future-of-se">The future</a></nav><button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode" aria-pressed="false"><span aria-hidden="true">☾</span></button></div></div></header>
<main class="wrap" id="top">
<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title"><p class="eyebrow">Meet the people behind the ideas</p><h1 id="hero-title">Good engineering starts with good questions.</h1><p class="copy">We’re a team of computer scientists exploring how to build software that works well, makes sense, and keeps people in the picture.</p><span class="pill">Agentic Software Engineering · Paderborn University</span></section>
<section class="section" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title"><p class="eyebrow">Five perspectives, one team</p><h2 id="team-title">The team</h2><p class="intro">We’re M.Sc. Computer Science students at the Agentic Software Engineering, Paderborn University lab. What connects us is curiosity about the systems behind software—and the people who depend on it. We bring different interests, from distributed infrastructure to human-computer interaction, to the same conversation.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">M</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Full-stack development, gaming</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without genuinely understanding system architecture or context. It works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough review.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">The core job will shift from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Autonomous agents may handle boilerplate, while engineers focus on verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p><div class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">N</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI assistants are productivity multipliers for well-understood domains and boilerplate, but struggle with complex legacy codebases. They can be confidently wrong about synchronization and concurrency, so a fast copilot still needs senior oversight.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineering will center on systems integration, prompt-driven formal verification, and distributed consensus rather than routine feature programming. Junior roles may evolve toward orchestration, where knowing failure modes and domain invariants is essential.</p><div class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">For a fresh developer, AI is an unbelievable interactive tutor that demystifies obscure compiler errors in seconds. Relying on it too early can create an illusion of competence: understanding output is not understanding mechanics.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Natural language and intent-driven wireframes may compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will focus on user empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state.</p><div class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">R</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">LLMs are incredible for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL or boilerplate endpoints. They lack deep situational context and may use deprecated methods without warning. Their utility is reducing initial friction, not making core architectural choices.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Developers may converse with multi-agent pipelines that build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting business constraints.</p><div class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</div></div></article>
<article class="card"><div class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">S</div><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping into engineering, lowering barriers to novel ideas. Software is also human communication and interface empathy. Current models produce functional code, but it can lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with design systems and ethical standards, freeing creative energy for user delight and seamless interactions.</p><div class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</div></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="section" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title"><p class="eyebrow">Our shared point of view</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2><div class="future" style="margin-top:1.25rem"><p class="lede">We see AI changing the mechanics of making software—not removing the need for engineering judgment. As agents take on boilerplate and routine implementation, human work becomes even more important: choosing the right problem, checking the result, and making systems work for people.</p><div class="principles"><article class="principle"><b>01 / DEFINE</b><h3>Make intent precise</h3><p>Requirements, edge cases, and domain constraints will guide capable coding tools.</p></article><article class="principle"><b>02 / VERIFY</b><h3>Earn trust with evidence</h3><p>Testing, formal methods, code review, and failure analysis remain essential.</p></article><article class="principle"><b>03 / CARE</b><h3>Keep people in the system</h3><p>Accessibility, privacy, thoughtful interfaces, and human oversight shape good software.</p></article></div><h3 class="forecast-title">Five lenses on what comes next</h3><div class="forecasts"><article class="forecast"><h4>Mohammad · Architecture and verification</h4><p>Agents may handle boilerplate, shifting engineers toward requirements, architecture, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p></article><article class="forecast"><h4>Nithya · Integration and invariants</h4><p>Systems integration, formal verification, distributed consensus, and failure modes become central concerns.</p></article><article class="forecast"><h4>Rafee · Interfaces from intent</h4><p>Intent-driven wireframes may produce accessible interfaces, giving engineers time for empathy and performant client state.</p></article><article class="forecast"><h4>Raghvendra · Multi-agent systems</h4><p>Agents may build and benchmark services while engineers define objectives, protect privacy, and reconcile constraints.</p></article><article class="forecast"><h4>Sayemah · Human-centered design</h4><p>Design systems and ethical standards can steer agents toward multidisciplinary, delightful experiences.</p></article></div></div></section>
</main>
<footer><div class="wrap"><p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · Context Engine</p></div></footer>
