      <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Our shared point of view</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2></div></div>
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p class="future-lede">We see AI changing the mechanics of making software—not removing the need for engineering judgment. As agents take on more boilerplate and routine implementation, the human work becomes even more important: choosing the right problem, checking the result, and making systems work for people.</p>
        <div class="principles">
          <article class="principle"><span class="principle-number">01 / DEFINE</span><h3>Make intent precise</h3><p>Clear requirements, edge cases, and domain constraints will guide increasingly capable coding tools.</p></article>
          <article class="principle"><span class="principle-number">02 / VERIFY</span><h3>Earn trust with evidence</h3><p>Testing, formal methods, code review, and failure analysis remain essential when generated code can look convincing.</p></article>
          <article class="principle"><span class="principle-number">03 / CARE</span><h3>Keep people in the system</h3><p>Accessibility, privacy, thoughtful interfaces, and human oversight should shape how we build—not just what we build.</p></article>
