<section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Agentic Software Engineering · Paderborn University</p><h1>Good engineering starts with good questions.</h1><p class="hero-copy">We’re a team of computer scientists exploring how to build software that works well, makes sense, and keeps people in the picture.</p><span class="pill">Five perspectives, one team</span></section>
<section class="section" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
<p class="eyebrow">Meet the people behind the ideas</p><h2 id="team-title">The team</h2>
<p class="intro">We’re M.Sc. Computer Science students at the Agentic Software Engineering, Paderborn University lab. Curiosity about software systems and the people who depend on them connects us across interests from distributed infrastructure to human-computer interaction.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Mohammad.png}}" alt="Mohammad"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Mohammad</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Full-stack development, gaming</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI excels at boilerplate and syntax lookups, but hallucinates subtle logical flaws and lacks architectural context. It works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough review.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Engineers will specify requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints while agents handle boilerplate. Verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build will be central.</p><p class="take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Nithya"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Nithya</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 7th semester, thesis stage</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, board games</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI multiplies productivity for familiar domains, but struggles with legacy systems and can be confidently wrong about synchronization and concurrency. Human oversight remains essential.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Systems integration, formal verification, and distributed consensus will matter more than routine feature work. Engineers will need to understand failure modes and domain invariants.</p><p class="take">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Rafee"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Rafee</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Frontend UI/UX, React/Next.js, table tennis, photography</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI is an interactive tutor for compiler errors, but relying on it too early risks understanding output without understanding mechanics.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Intent-driven wireframes may compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers will focus on empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state.</p><p class="take">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Raghvendra"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Raghvendra</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 3rd semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, chess</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">LLMs are useful for prototyping and SQL but lack situational context and may use deprecated methods. Their value is reducing friction, not making architectural choices.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Multi-agent pipelines may build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting constraints.</p><p class="take">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Sayemah"><div class="content"><div class="name"><h3>Sayemah</h3><span class="semester">M.Sc. · 1st semester</span></div><p class="interests"><b>Interests:</b> Calligraphy, culinary arts and cooking, traveling, human-computer interaction</p><h4>Fun fact</h4><p class="detail">I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p class="detail">AI brings creativity and swift prototyping, but software also needs human communication and interface empathy. Functional code can lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p><h4>Where software engineering will be in 5–10 years</h4><p class="detail">Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with design systems and ethical standards, making room for user delight.</p><p class="take">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</p></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="section" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title"><p class="eyebrow">Our shared perspective</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2><div class="future"><p class="future-copy">AI will change how software is made, not remove the need for engineering judgment. As agents take on routine implementation, engineers will define the problem, verify results, and ensure systems serve people.</p><div class="future-grid"><article><b>Define intent</b><p>Requirements, edge cases, and domain constraints guide capable tools.</p></article><article><b>Verify with evidence</b><p>Testing, formal methods, code review, and failure analysis remain essential.</p></article><article><b>Keep people central</b><p>Accessibility, privacy, thoughtful interfaces, and human oversight shape good software.</p></article><article><b>Build resilient systems</b><p>Integration, distributed systems, orchestration, and fault tolerance will matter.</p></article></div></div></section>
</main>
